  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will remain in place over the region through \ntonight. Clear skies will allow for efficient radiational \ncooling and expect lows in the upper 40s and lower 50s. A few \nspots across interior NE PA may manage to drip into the low to \nmid 40s if cooling ends up being more efficient than currently \nanticipated. This will be the last chilly night of the workweek \nbefore temperatures warm up Tuesday onwards.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nAfter a brief break in the unsettled weather pattern, multiple\nrounds of showers and thunderstorms will likely impact the area\nlate Tuesday through at least Thursday. The first round of\nshowers and thunderstorms will arrive behind a warm front and \nwith a pre-frontal trough Tuesday evening. There is a small \nchance of a couple of stronger storms Tuesday evening into \nTuesday night, although most CAMs suggest that the best \ninstability will be to the west of the local area and storms \nwill be coming in during a diurnally unfavorable time of day. \nThe severe weather risk may be a little bit higher if storms end\nup moving in earlier than currently anticipated, but either way\nbulk shear values of around 30 knots supports an SPC Marginal \nRisk (Level 1 of 5) of severe weather with damaging wind gusts\nbeing the main concern.\n\nFrom there, a cold front will push southeast across at least a\nportion of the area before slowing and wavering over the region\nWednesday through late Thursday. This will result in periodic \nshowers and thunderstorms for the mid to late week timeframe. \nThere may be opportunities for a few stronger storms given the \nproximity of the front, but there is still uncertainty in the \nexact placement of the boundary and the amount of instability in\nplace each afternoon. Will need to keep an eye on the forecast \nand update as needed. \n\nIt will also be quite humid and warm across the area with highs\nin the 80s and dew points in the upper 60s and low to mid 70s \nWednesday and Thursday. PWAT values will increase to 1.5 to at \nleast 2 inches during this time and flow parallel to the \nboundary may result in potential in heavy rainfall rates and \ntraining showers/thunderstorms.",
